17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 16"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",17," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"Classic Synchronization Problems"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"Three puzzles that teach the patterns hiding inside every queue, cache, and lock you will ever write"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"By now you have the tools: a mutex to guard a critical section, counting semaphores to count and wait for resources, condition variables to sleep until some predicate becomes true. Tools are not the same as judgement, though. The hard part of concurrency is never \"which primitive exists\", it is recognising, in the tangle of a real system, which pattern you are actually looking at, and wiring the primitives up so the pattern is correct under every possible interleaving."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"That is what the three classic problems in this chapter are for. Producer–Consumer, Readers–Writers, and Dining Philosophers are not museum pieces or interview trivia. Each one distils a recurring shape of coordination into its purest form, strips away the application noise, and forces you to reason about the corner cases with nothing to hide behind. Learn them properly and you will start seeing them everywhere, in a thread pool, a database lock manager, a message broker, a read-through cache, an in-memory queue between two goroutines."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"We will state each problem, build a correct solution out of the primitives you already know, and then, the part most textbooks skip, connect it straight to the systems you build and operate. We will also let the last problem, Dining Philosophers, walk us right up to the edge of deadlock, which is the subject of the next chapters. This chapter is where synchronization stops being a list of primitives and becomes a way of seeing."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-why-classic-problems",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-why-classic-problems","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Why we study three ancient puzzles"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-producer-consumer",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-producer-consumer","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":["$L21","$L22"]}]}],"$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]}]]}],"$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]
